{
  "dom_id": "step-q140561",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{61}} & \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{61}} \\\\ \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{61}} & \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{61}}\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{13} & 2 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind $|\\text{det}(PQ)|$ given that $P$ is an orthogonal matrix.",
  "worked_solution": "We use the following facts: \n\n- The determinant of a product is equal to the product of the determinants.\n\n- The determinant of an orthogonal matrix $P$ is equal to $\\pm 1.$\n\nApplying these ideas, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\text{det}(PQ)| & =|\\text{det}(P)\\cdot \\text{det}(Q)| \\\\  & =|(\\pm 1)\\cdot \\text{det}(Q)| \\\\  & =|\\pm 1|\\cdot |\\text{det}(Q)| \\\\  & =|\\text{det}(Q)| \\\\  & =|\\sqrt{13}\\cdot \\sqrt{5}-0\\cdot 2| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{65}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
